# assets/hero.tape.in — the ONE hand-authored body behind every repo's README hero.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE. The tapes are GENERATED from it by
# scripts/gen-hero-tape.sh, one per row of assets/hero-repos.txt, and
# audit-core.sh §9j reds if a rendered tape drifts from this template.
# Edit HERE, then run `make gen-hero-tape`.
#
# WHY A TEMPLATE. assets/README.md already claimed the property that makes this
# possible — "re-run the command after any prompt or tooling change and the hero
# updates, no manual re-recording" — and then the fleet had exactly one hero, shot
# inside the wrong repo (#698). Nine hand-recorded gifs would drift the way the
# theme (#679), the alias tables (#685) and the porting matrix (#686) drifted; the
# per-repo delta here is two lines, so it is a substitution, not nine files.
#
# PLACEHOLDERS, substituted by the generator. Everything else is verbatim:
#   @@HEADER@@    the generated-file provenance banner
#   @@THEME@@     `Set Theme { … }`, rendered from theme/palette.toml
#   @@CHECKOUT@@  the repo's checkout path — the `cd` in the hidden setup
#   @@SIGCMD@@    the ONE command that is this repo's own (Core: `core status`;
#                 an OS repo: `up -n`, which resolves to that distro's manager)
#   @@SIGNOTE@@   the trailing comment on that line — a TAPE comment, never in the gif
#   @@HOSTGUARD@@ the precondition: for an OS row, that the RENDERING HOST is the distro
#                 the row is about. Hidden, so it costs the viewer nothing.
#   @@PROOF@@     the line that makes the resolution VISIBLE: `up -n` prints "via zypper",
#                 the manager and not the verb, so it alone cannot show `dup` rather than
#                 `up` — the distinction #698 is about. This prints the resolved command.
#   @@SIGWAIT@@   how long the tape holds after the signature command: the registry's
#                 optional seventh column, 4s when a row has none.
#
# KEEP IT SHORT — the budget is ~15 s. But §9k caps BYTES, not seconds, and the two are
# only loosely related: adding a command that repaints the screen costs far more than
# adding a second of stillness. Weigh a new tour step by what it redraws.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@@HEADER@@

Output assets/demo.gif

Set Shell "zsh"
Set FontFamily "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font"   # must be a Nerd Font, or icons break
Set FontSize 20
Set Width 1200
Set Height 700
Set Padding 20
@@THEME@@
Set TypingSpeed 45ms
# VHS captures at 50fps by default, which is twice what a terminal recording needs and
# roughly twice the bytes. THE FRAME COUNT IS THE COST, not the clip length: the first
# shortened cut ran ~13 s against the old ~25 s and came out BIGGER (2.46 MB vs 1.84 MB),
# because GIF pays per changed pixel and this tour has four full-screen colour redraws
# where the old one had pager quits and a `clear`. Sleeps on a static screen are nearly
# free; frames during a redraw are not.
Set Framerate 24

# --- silent setup: land on a clean prompt, with nothing that can page ---
# CORE_NO_PAGER and GIT_PAGER are what let the tour drop the four `q` keystrokes
# the first cut needed: a pager costs a Type, an Enter and two Sleeps per command,
# which is most of the difference between a ~27 s clip and this one.
#
# @@HOSTGUARD@@ IS THE SAME IDEA ONE LEVEL UP. Core reads the capability declaration ONCE,
# at shell startup, from the HOST's linked ~/.config/zsh/os.capabilities
# (zsh/02-capabilities.zsh) — `cd`-ing into a repo does not switch it, and neither does
# `up -n`, which probes $PATH. So an OS repo's hero filmed on the WRONG BOX records that
# box's package manager under a comment naming the row's: render dotfiles-Fedora on a
# MacBook and the gif says `brew upgrade` while the tape says `dnf`. Matching-host rendering
# was an unstated assumption; this makes it a checked precondition, and a mismatched host
# fails the render instead of publishing a hero that contradicts itself.
#
# `|| exit 1` ON THE cd IS LOAD-BEARING. This block is hidden, so a `cd` to a path that
# does not exist on the rendering box prints its error into frames nobody records and
# then films $HOME — a hero of the wrong tree, which is #698's original defect wearing a
# different hat. Exiting kills the session instead, so vhs fails visibly rather than
# producing a plausible-looking gif of the wrong directory.
#
# DOTFILES_NO_AUTOTMUX=1 IS THE THIRD PRECONDITION, and it must be exported BEFORE the
# `source`. Every OS layer auto-attaches tmux for an interactive TTY, and the shell vhs
# drives IS one — so on an unguarded host the `source` line below attaches (or creates)
# a `main` session and never returns; every keystroke after it lands in that pane, the
# `cd` never happens, and the gif films a tmux status bar over whatever directory the pane
# had. That is how the first #877 render came out. The knob is the fleet's ONE opt-out
# name (MacBook, openSUSE and Gentoo honoured it first; #877 made it all seven), and
# scripts/gen-hero-tape.sh refuses to render a row whose shell layer auto-attaches without
# honouring it — so the check is at GENERATION time, against the layer's source.
#
# WHY NO `[[ -z $TMUX ]] || exit 1` AFTER THE source, mirroring the cd: if the knob were
# ignored, that line would be typed INTO the attached pane, where $TMUX is set — and
# `exit 1` there closes a shell in the user's real session. The generator-time check has
# no such failure mode, which is why it lives there and not here.
Hide
Type "export CORE_NO_PAGER=1 GIT_PAGER=cat DOTFILES_NO_AUTOTMUX=1" Enter
Type "source ~/.config/zsh/.zshrc" Enter
Type "cd @@CHECKOUT@@ || exit 1" Enter
Type "@@HOSTGUARD@@" Enter
Type "clear" Enter
Show
Sleep 1500ms

# --- the tour: three shared marquee moments, then the two that are this repo's ---
Type "ll" Sleep 400ms Enter Sleep 1600ms             # eza — icons + git status
Type "cat README.md" Sleep 400ms Enter Sleep 1600ms  # bat — syntax highlighting
Type "glog -8" Sleep 400ms Enter Sleep 1600ms        # git log, graphed and decorated
# @@SIGWAIT@@, not the 2.2 s the other steps get: the signature command is the one that does
# WORK, and how long it works is the archive's. Fedora's dry run is `dnf --refresh check-update`,
# which re-validates repo metadata against the mirror before it answers — ~3 s on a warm cache
# — and at 2.2 s the first #948 render typed the proof line INTO the spinner and cut before the
# answer. Portage resolves `emerge --pretend` in ~10 s. So the wait is a registry column: 4 s
# unless the row says otherwise (hero-repos.txt `sigwait`). Stillness on a static screen is
# nearly free in bytes; a truncated answer is not free at all.
Type "@@SIGCMD@@" Sleep 400ms Enter Sleep @@SIGWAIT@@  # @@SIGNOTE@@
Type "@@PROOF@@" Sleep 400ms Enter Sleep 2200ms
Sleep 1s
